# Copy to .env in the checkout that needs it. It is untracked and survives
# `git reset --hard`, which is how each checkout on this host keeps its own identity
# across deploys. One machine serves all three roles; the difference is this file.

# --- Production: /srv/website, serving ivangetsitdone.com --------------------------
# scripts/bootstrap.sh writes the first lines and deploy.yml maintains the rest. Never
# add COMPOSE_FILE here; the deploy strips it every run anyway, because a dev overlay
# layered onto the live stack would bind-mount a working tree over production.
# SITE_ADDRESS=ivangetsitdone.com
# WWW_ADDRESS=www.ivangetsitdone.com
# PREVIEW_ADDRESS=preview.ivangetsitdone.com
# DEV_ADDRESS=dev.ivangetsitdone.com
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=website
# APP_IMAGE=ghcr.io/ivangetsitdone/website:<sha>

# --- The development site: /srv/website-dev, serving dev.ivangetsitdone.com -------
# Runs compose.dev.yaml alone: one container, no Caddy, no published ports. Production's
# Caddy proxies it on the dev-app alias. APP_IMAGE supplies the dependencies; the source
# comes from this tree. Refresh it with `docker compose pull` after a dependency change.
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=website-dev
# COMPOSE_FILE=compose.dev.yaml
# APP_IMAGE=ghcr.io/ivangetsitdone/website:<sha>

# --- A clone, or CI, with no DNS --------------------------------------------------
# Every named site Caddy is given needs a certificate, and neither a laptop nor a runner
# can answer a challenge for these names, so they get addresses instead.
# SITE_ADDRESS=:80
# WWW_ADDRESS=:8080
# PREVIEW_ADDRESS=:8081
# DEV_ADDRESS=:8083
